2. NON-NEGOTIABLE PROJECT CONSTRAINTS

Follow these requirements throughout development.

2.1 Software-only implementation

Build CivicSync entirely as a software application.

Do not require or introduce:

IoT devices.
Physical sensors.
Dedicated hardware.
Raspberry Pi or Arduino.
Machine learning models.
Large language models.
Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG).
AI agents.
Paid AI APIs.
Computer vision.
Unnecessary blockchain infrastructure.

Use conventional software engineering, database queries, deterministic business rules, configurable categories, validation, filtering, aggregation, and analytics.

For example, classify a civic report using a predefined category selected by the citizen. Assign a department using a configurable category-to-department mapping. Calculate statistics using database queries.

Do not pretend that rule-based classification is machine learning.

2.2 No fake functionality

Every visible interactive control must perform its intended action or clearly indicate that the feature is unavailable.

Do not create buttons that do nothing.

Do not use hardcoded dashboard values as if they were live database results.

Do not display a successful submission message unless the record has actually been saved.

Do not simulate an administrator's approval when no approval operation occurred.

Do not claim that an email, SMS, push notification, or external message was delivered unless the corresponding integration confirms delivery.

If an external service requires credentials, build the integration boundary, configuration instructions, and clear status handling. Never expose credentials in frontend code.

2.3 No duplicated dashboards or role features

Each role must have its own dashboard, navigation, permissions, information hierarchy, and primary actions.

Do not simply duplicate the same dashboard four times and change the heading.

Do not repeat entire feature modules across role dashboards.

Shared data can appear in different forms when required by the user's responsibilities. For example, a citizen sees their own report status, while an administrator sees the overall report queue. These are distinct workflows.

2.4 Existing project preservation

If source code already exists:

Inspect the existing repository and its directory structure.
Identify the framework, routes, components, authentication system, database, and existing features.
Reuse working code where appropriate.
Repair broken functionality before introducing unnecessary replacements.
Preserve working integrations and existing useful functionality.
Avoid creating duplicate components, conflicting route definitions, and multiple competing authentication systems.
Update dependencies only when necessary.
Never delete working features merely to make implementation easier.

If the project is new, establish the architecture described below.

3. PRODUCT VISION AND PROBLEM STATEMENT
3.1 Problem

Public information is frequently distributed through different communication channels, including social media, messaging services, websites, SMS, radio, and departmental announcements.

When messages are inconsistent, outdated, difficult to verify, or spread across different channels, citizens may struggle to identify which information is current and trustworthy.

Civic issues such as damaged roads, overflowing waste bins, broken streetlights, drainage problems, water supply interruptions, and public safety concerns also frequently require residents to contact different departments and follow separate processes.

Citizens may have difficulty answering basic questions:

Has anyone received my complaint?
Which department is responsible?
Has the problem been assigned?
Is someone working on it?
Has the issue actually been resolved?
Where can I find verified public alerts?

Authorities also need a structured method to review reports, assign responsibilities, communicate progress, maintain records, and analyze recurring issues.

3.2 CivicSync solution

CivicSync combines two complementary capabilities:

Capability A: Unified Public Alerts

Provide a central platform where authorized organizations can create, verify, publish, update, and archive public alerts.

Citizens can browse alerts, filter them by location and category, view their details, and access a consistent source of information.

Capability B: Civic Issue Reporting and Resolution

Allow citizens to report local problems with descriptions, categories, locations, and supporting images.

Authorized organization administrators review the submissions, assign reports to responsible field staff, monitor progress, and verify closure.

Citizens can track the lifecycle of their reports and receive notifications about meaningful status changes.

Supporting capabilities
Role-based dashboards.
Geographic issue visualization.
Department assignment.
Report filtering and search.
Notification history.
Resolution evidence.
Audit trails.
Operational analytics.
Exportable reports.
Account and organization management.

CivicSync must not claim to replace every government communication channel. Its initial purpose is to provide a unified, traceable platform for alerts and civic issue management.

